WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:01.550
Pour a bucket of water down a

2
00:00:01.550 --> 00:00:03.350
hill, and it races down,

3
00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:05.219
taking soil with it.

4
00:00:05.219 --> 00:00:07.237
Now cut the hill into steps.

5
00:00:07.637 --> 00:00:08.937
On a steep slope,

6
00:00:08.937 --> 00:00:10.656
rain runs off fast.

7
00:00:10.656 --> 00:00:12.269
It carries away the topsoil,

8
00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:14.011
the thin, rich layer that crops need

9
00:00:14.011 --> 00:00:15.067
most.

10
00:00:15.067 --> 00:00:17.076
A few centimetres can take centuries to

11
00:00:17.076 --> 00:00:17.968
form.

12
00:00:18.368 --> 00:00:20.493
Terraces turn the slope into a staircase

13
00:00:20.493 --> 00:00:22.130
of flat fields.

14
00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:23.175
On each step,

15
00:00:23.175 --> 00:00:24.742
water slows down and soaks in,

16
00:00:24.742 --> 00:00:26.981
and the soil stays where it is.

17
00:00:27.381 --> 00:00:29.471
Farmers in hills across Asia have built

18
00:00:29.471 --> 00:00:31.294
terraces for centuries.

19
00:00:31.294 --> 00:00:33.546
In India they're common in the western

20
00:00:33.546 --> 00:00:35.297
and central Himalayas.

21
00:00:35.697 --> 00:00:37.589
There are cousins of the same idea.

22
00:00:37.589 --> 00:00:38.971
Ploughing across the slope,

23
00:00:38.971 --> 00:00:40.805
along the contour lines.

24
00:00:40.805 --> 00:00:42.930
Strips of grass between crops.

25
00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:44.451
And rows of trees that break the

26
00:00:44.451 --> 00:00:45.331
wind.

27
00:00:45.732 --> 00:00:46.626
Without such care,

28
00:00:46.626 --> 00:00:49.157
land is lost to gullies and ravines,

29
00:00:49.157 --> 00:00:51.280
like those of the Chambal valley.

30
00:00:51.679 --> 00:00:52.782
For GS Three:

31
00:00:52.782 --> 00:00:54.082
name the agent of erosion,

32
00:00:54.082 --> 00:00:55.777
then match it with the fix.

33
00:00:55.777 --> 00:00:57.217
Terraces for slopes,

34
00:00:57.217 --> 00:00:58.899
shelter belts for wind.

